                <pb facs="0285" n="187" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg187"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002 Manchuria/215: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-07-27T18:00:00+08:00">July 27, 1932—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 27—7:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>861. Following from American Consul General at Nanking: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“July 26, 11 a.m. Department’s July 17, 11 p.m. to the Legation. I
                            delivered message to the Minister of Finance today. He asked me to
                            inform you that he deeply appreciated thoughtful reply from the
                            Department, his Government would as hitherto give special heed to the
                            principle set forth in section 9 of the Department’s telegram. He
                            stated, however, that financial difficulties are increasing owing to the
                            decrease of customs revenues which for July will fall to less than
                            10,000,000 taels or half average monthly revenue last year.[”]</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
